# Stat card

A metric tile: muted label, large value, optional change badge and
leading icon. Composed from the card and badge partials.

<%= embed :stat_card, :with_icon %>

## When to use

- Dashboard headline numbers: open referrals, episodes this week,
  average wait.
- A row of three or four at the top of a section, gridded with
  `gap-4`.

## When not to use

- Numbers with structure — comparisons across categories or time
  belong in a `table` (or a chart, decided per feature).
- Text content — a stat card presents one number; prose belongs in
  a `card`.
- Many metrics — past ~4 tiles the dashboard stops being
  glanceable; promote the detail into a table.

## Usage

`render_stat_card` (`Gesso::Components::StatCardHelper`) validates `change_type`
and renders the tile.

```erb
<%%= render_stat_card(title: "Open referrals", value: 42) %>
```

Add a change badge and a leading icon in the same call:

```erb
<%%= render_stat_card(
  title: "Active referrals",
  value: "342",
  change: "+12%",
  change_type: "positive",
  icon: "users"
) %>
```

## Change types

The `change` badge is coloured by `change_type` — by *meaning*, not by
the direction of the number.

<%= embed :stat_card, :variants %>

| Change type | When to use |
| ----------- | ----------- |
| `positive` | A metric that improved — shown as a success badge. |
| `negative` | A metric that worsened — shown as a destructive badge. |
| `neutral` (default) | A change shown without judgement — a secondary badge. |

## Options

| Param | Notes |
| ----- | ----- |
| `title` | metric label, rendered muted |
| `value` | the number, rendered large |
| `change` | optional change text ("+12%"); omitted when nil |
| `change_type` | see **Change types** above |
| `icon` | optional `icons/` svg name in a tinted box |
| `classes` | extra classes on the card |

## Do and don't

- Do say the period in the title when there is one: "Referrals
  (7 days)" — a bare number invites misreading.
- Do pick `change_type` by *meaning*: falling wait times are
  `positive` even though the number went down.
- Don't show `change` without a baseline the user knows; "+12%"
  against an unknown period is noise.
- Don't use stat cards as links-in-disguise; if it navigates, make
  the action explicit elsewhere.

## Accessibility and content

- Value and label are plain text in source order (label, then
  value), so screen readers announce "Open referrals, 42".
- The change badge carries its sign in text ("+12%"), not only in
  colour — the badge rules apply (see *Badge*).
- `tabular-nums` keeps rows of stat cards aligned (see
  *Typography*).
